Revert my header menu restyling: it looked worse and I could not explain why

Kenneth reported the printer menu looking wrong. Over three attempts I gave
three explanations, changed the code to match each one, and made it worse
every time. This puts every value back to what it was before I touched it.

What I got wrong:

I claimed plain wxDC draws unantialiased on macOS and moved the popup's
border onto a graphics context to "match the rest of the header". That is
false -- wxOSX implements wxDC on top of wxGCDC, so both paths were already
antialiased and the rasterizer never changed. All my change did was shift the
stroke half a pixel outward, onto the edge of the window's shape mask.

I then claimed the mask was eating the stroke there. The mask is genuinely
hard-edged (DoSetPathShape fills with wxANTIALIAS_NONE and reduces to a 1-bit
region), so the mechanism is real -- but when I finally measured it, it costs
one part in a thousand of the border's ink. Invisible. Not the symptom.

I then found that full-width rows repaint the border away down both sides,
which is true and is caused by my own change from inset rows to flush rows.
Fixing it did not fix what Kenneth was seeing either.

So I do not know what actually made it look bad, and I stopped guessing.

